    "en": "When Wayne was a teenager, his mother hired Mrs. Brown to do the ironing, and Wayne's job was to deliver and pick up the clothes. Mrs. Brown had milk jugs filled with colored liquid, with tubes leading down to a basin of water. When Wayne asked, she explained it was wine inside; when the bubbles stopped coming up through the water, the wine was finished. Wayne uses this small kitchen detail to teach a serious doctrine. New wine bubbles because the sugar in the juice is alive — fermentation is a sign of life. Living faith behaves the same way: it is expansive, it stretches its container, it does not sit politely inside whatever religious shape the disciple grew up in. A rigid container cannot hold living wine; a still faith is a finished faith. The disciple who is no longer being stretched has settled into an old wineskin and is, in Wayne's framing, finished in a way Jesus did not intend.",
